    "topic_code": "1.7",
    "topic_title": "Selecting Procedures for Determining Limits",
    "stem": "Which procedure is appropriate for determining lim x→2 |x−2|/(x−2)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Replace |x−2| with x−2 on both sides of 2, then cancel to obtain a limit of 1.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Absolute-value removal error: |x−2| equals x−2 only when x ≥ 2. For x < 2, |x−2| = −(x−2), so this cancellation ignores the left-hand behavior. Fix: Write the absolute-value expression separately for x < 2 and x > 2."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Check the left-hand and right-hand limits separately, using the appropriate expression for |x−2| on each side.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The absolute value requires different expressions on the two sides of 2. For x < 2, the quotient is −1; for x > 2, it is 1, so the unequal one-sided limits show that the two-sided limit does not exist."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Use direct substitution and interpret 0/0 as a limit of 0.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Indeterminate-form error: 0/0 is not a number and does not determine the limit. Direct substitution therefore does not resolve this problem. Fix: When substitution gives 0/0, examine the expression for nearby inputs."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Use the denominator’s approach to 0 to conclude that the limit is ∞.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Zero-denominator shortcut: a denominator approaching 0 does not by itself imply an infinite limit. Here, the numerator also approaches 0, and the quotient remains either −1 or 1 for x ≠ 2. Fix: Check the numerator and simplify the quotient on each side before concluding infinite behavior."
     }
    ]
   },
